# Normalise line endings explicitly rather than relying on each machine's
# core.autocrlf.  Without this, a Windows working tree holds CRLF while the Linux
# CI runner holds LF.
#
# This is about committed *data* determinism, not bundle hashes: measured, the
# built JS and worker chunks are byte-identical across the two platforms.  The
# only bundle difference is the emitted CSS, and that comes from Tailwind's
# platform-native binary rounding a float differently (lab(...) 60.7449 on Windows
# vs 60.745 on Linux) -- not from line endings.  Recorded here so nobody re-chases
# it.  The real gain below is the GeoJSON's trailing newline, which otherwise
# differs by a byte depending on which machine regenerated it.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Committed data and images are generated artefacts, so the data file is pinned to
# LF rather than left to whatever Python's text mode produced, and binaries are
# marked so git never rewrites a byte of them.
*.geojson text eol=lf
*.png -text
*.wasm -text
